# Git commit messages

When creating git commits (only when the user asks):

- **One line only** — subject line, no body, no blank line, no bullet lists
- **Conventional Commits** — `type: imperative summary` (lowercase type, no scope required)
- **Imperative mood** — "add feature" not "added feature"
- **Short** — aim for ≤72 characters; drop filler words
- **No period** at the end of the subject line

## Allowed types

`feat`, `fix`, `refactor`, `chore`, `build`, `ci`, `docs`, `test`, `perf`, `style`

Use the type that best matches the change. Prefer `fix` for bugs, `feat` for user-visible behavior, `chore` for housekeeping.

## Examples

```
feat: add P99 Login Proxy import and update checking
fix: prevent crash when checking updates from menu bar
build: stamp dev version from git at link time
ci: replace auto build workflow with manual release workflow
chore: rebrand web admin as alfred-identity-backend
refactor: centralize app name and config directory
docs: document SSO_SOURCE_NAME for Discord /sso get
test: cover discord command group restrictions
```

## Avoid

```
# Bad — multi-line body
feat: add import modal

Adds discovery and folder picker.

# Bad — no type
Added update checking

# Bad — past tense
feat: added update checking
```
